[[ ## response ## ]]
To create a secure login form, you must prioritize server-side processing while using the client-side code for user experience. 

* The HTML structure uses an ID for the form and specific input types to ensure validation occurs before submission.
* The JavaScript uses the Fetch API to send credentials via an HTTP POST request to your backend endpoint.
* You must ensure your server implements robust password hashing (like Argon2 or bcrypt) and protects against CSRF and SQL injection.
* Always enforce HTTPS to encrypt data in transit between the client and server.

```html
<form id="loginForm">
  <input type="text" id="username" required placeholder="Username">
  <input type="password" id="password" required placeholder="Password">
  <button type="submit">Login</button>
</form>
<div id="errorMessage"></div>

<script>
document.getElementById('loginForm').addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const data = { 
    username: document.getElementById('username').value, 
    password: document.getElementById('password').value 
  };
  try {
    const response = await fetch('/api/login', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify(data)
    });
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Invalid credentials');
    window.location.href = '/dashboard';
  } catch (err) {
    document.getElementById('errorMessage').innerText = err.message;
  }
});
</script>
```

